<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Android 12+ (API 31+). The API 26-30 twin is full_backup_content.xml, and the two
  must be kept in step — minSdk is 26, so both are live.

  The Spotify bearer secrets do not leave the device by this route. Credentials.kt
  states the rule ("never in the records manifest, never in an export, never in a
  backup"); this is where the backup half of it is actually enforced.

  A refresh token does not expire on its own, so a copy in a Google Drive backup — or
  in an `adb backup` pulled off an API 26-30 device — mints Spotify access tokens as
  the user until they think to revoke the app, which they have no reason to do.

  Excluded by file, because that is the only granularity backup rules have. That is
  the whole reason the credentials live in a DataStore of their own: `settings` keeps
  the friends list and the setlist.fm username, which are identity rather than
  credential and are exactly what someone reinstalling wants back.

  cloud and device are both excluded: device-to-device transfer carries the same file.
-->
<data-extraction-rules>
    <cloud-backup>
        <exclude domain="file" path="datastore/credentials.preferences_pb" />
    </cloud-backup>
    <device-transfer>
        <exclude domain="file" path="datastore/credentials.preferences_pb" />
    </device-transfer>
</data-extraction-rules>
